WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.160
A session identifier is the handle that lets the application connect a request to a session.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:12.580
In a traditional server-side model, the identifier is stored by the client, often in a cookie, and the server uses it to look up session state.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:21.600
That state may include the user ID, authentication time, roles, tenant, security flags, expiration, and other context.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:29.500
Because possession of the identifier can allow a request to be treated as authenticated, session identifiers must be protected like credentials.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:36.600
Secure identifiers need strong randomness, unpredictability, uniqueness, and enough length to resist guessing.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:43.140
Developers should rely on mature framework or platform session generation rather than inventing their own identifier format.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:50.160
Identifiers should not reveal user IDs, timestamps, roles, tenant names, or other meaningful internals.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:54.620
They should not be sequential, human-readable, or derived from predictable input.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:00.200
A session ID should be an opaque reference, not a miniature profile of the user or application.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.020
Session tokens and bearer tokens carry similar responsibility.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.680
If a token grants access, then whoever presents it may be treated as authorized within its scope.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:17.500
That means storage, transmission, logging, expiration, rotation, and revocation all matter.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:25.000
Avoid placing session identifiers in URLs, log messages, analytics tools, referrer headers, or error details.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:37.140
Server-side validation remains essential: the application should confirm that the session exists, is active, has not expired, matches required security state, and is still allowed for the requested action.
